    "problem_statement": "Fill function swaps key-value pairs in dictionaries\n\nWhen using the `Fill` function with dictionary specs, the keys and values are being swapped in the output. This appears to affect dictionary transformations where the expected key-value mapping is reversed.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom glom import glom, Fill, T\n\n# Simple dictionary fill - keys and values are swapped\nresult = glom('abc', Fill((T[0], {T[1]: T[2]})))\nprint(\"Expected: ('a', {'b': 'c'})\")\nprint(f\"Actual: {result}\")\n\n# Another example with sets\nresult2 = glom('123', Fill({T[0], frozenset([T[1], T[2]])}))\nprint(\"Expected: {'1', frozenset(['2', '3'])}\")  \nprint(f\"Actual: {result2}\")\n```\n\nThe dictionary `{T[1]: T[2]}` should map `'b'` to `'c'`, but instead it's creating `{'c': 'b'}`.\n\n#### Additional Context\n\nThis also affects more complex nested structures and recursive references that use Fill with dictionary specifications. The key-value reversal breaks expected data transformations."
  }
